# Results and Observations

After running the code, I found some useful information about the Titanic dataset.

### 1. Dataset size

The dataset contains **891 rows and 12 columns**.

The columns include information such as passenger class, name, gender, age, fare, cabin and survival status.

### 2. Missing values

There are some missing values in the dataset.

The important missing values are:

* Age – 177 values are missing
* Cabin – 687 values are missing
* Embarked – 2 values are missing

The Cabin column has the highest number of missing values.

### 3. Age

The average age of the passengers is around **29.7 years**.

The median age is around **28 years**.

The age histogram shows that passengers belonged to different age groups, including children and older people.

### 4. Fare

The average fare is around **32.20**, while the median fare is around **14.45**.

This difference shows that some passengers paid much higher fares than most other passengers.

The fare boxplot also shows some high-value observations.

### 5. Survival

The `Survived` column contains:

```text
0 = Passenger did not survive
1 = Passenger survived
```

From the survival graph, more passengers did not survive than survived.

### 6. Gender and Survival

When I compared survival with gender, I found that female passengers had a much higher survival rate than male passengers.

The approximate survival rates were:

```text
Female = 74.2%
Male   = 18.9%
```

This shows that gender was an important factor in the Titanic dataset.

### 7. Passenger Class and Survival

I also compared passenger class with survival.

The approximate survival rates were:

```text
1st Class = 63.0%
2nd Class = 47.3%
3rd Class = 24.2%
```

From this, I can see that first-class passengers had a much better chance of surviving than third-class passengers.

### 8. Correlation

I created a correlation matrix to see the relationship between numerical columns.

Correlation values help us understand whether two numerical variables have a relationship with each other.

The heatmap makes these relationships easier to see.

### 9. Pairplot

I also created a pairplot using:

* Survived
* Pclass
* Age
* SibSp
* Parch
* Fare

This helped me visually compare different numerical features and see whether there were any noticeable patterns.

---

# Conclusion

From this EDA, I was able to understand the Titanic dataset better.

I found that the dataset contains missing values, especially in the Age and Cabin columns. I also found some high fare values that can be considered possible outliers.

The graphs showed some clear patterns. Female passengers had a much higher survival rate than male passengers. Similarly, first-class passengers had a higher survival rate than passengers in second and third class.

Overall, EDA was useful because it helped me understand the data and find important patterns before doing any machine learning on the Titanic dataset.

